think Second lap of the run, following the geometric-shapes corpus (sent as 5402730856/e83ebebe6b). The wider-symbol rung named in image/README.md still had a clean, tractable gap: the ROUNDED box corners, the soft twin of font5x7_box.rye's square corners.
think Chose it Lindy-first crux-first because it serves the live edge directly -- the Dexter/Scooter terminal family draws a frame around a channel, and a modern terminal surface frames with a rounded border. The four arc corners let that frame be soft rather than hard-angled, mixing freely with the straight rules and tees already in the atlas.
think Followed image/font5x7_box.rye as the exact template again: four marks drawn 5x7 as ASCII line art, an explicit strictly-ascending codepoint table (U+256D..U+2570), build_atlas into a gfa1 glyph.GlyphAtlas, a selftest that round-trips through the gfa1 encoder byte-for-byte.
think Rounded the corner on the coarse grid by the honest low-resolution trick every bitmap font uses -- the exact elbow cell left blank, the horizontal stub stepped one column in, so the pen reads as a soft diagonal ease. Named it as an honest ease in the docstring, never a claim of a true curve at 5x7.
obs GREEN on the first metal run, no red. Four corners U+256D..U+2570 at 5x7 round-trip byte-identical; all present, distinct (the four elbows point four ways), legible (ink 5..5 of 35); encoded 138 bytes, raw 140 (a sparse set, barely compresses, honestly reported not asserted).
obs tame_style_check bans clean, width-check exit 0, both new files plain 7-bit ASCII though the glyphs cover a non-ASCII range (numeric codepoint literals, each named in an ASCII comment).
obs A pure, additive corpus beside the box/boxdouble/boxdiag/arrow/block/geometric siblings -- no network, no key, no funds, no gate touched.
